      "problem": "If $p$ and $q$ are primes, $\\text{gcd}(a,p)=1$ and $p\\,|\\,a{q}^{3},$ then\n\n{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that for any integers $a,$ $b,$ and $c,$\n\n$c\\,|\\,ab\\,$ and $\\,\\text{gcd}(a,c)=1$ $\\,⟹\\,$ $\\,c\\,|\\,b.$\n\nAlso, given that $p$ and $q$ are primes, we get\n\n${p}^{m}\\,|\\,{q}^{n}$ $\\,⟹\\,$ $p=q,$\n\nwhere $m$ and $n$ are positive integers.\n\nWith that in mind, we proceed as follows:\n\n- Since $\\text{gcd}(a,p)=1$ and $p\\,|\\,a{q}^{3},$ we must obtain that\n\n$$\np\\,|\\,{q}^{3}.\n$$\n\n- Since $p$ and $q$ are primes and $p\\,|\\,{q}^{3},$ we get\n\n$$\nq=p.\n$$",
